F1, MEDIUM, AND THE ONLY CODE DEFECT: cnc-ddraw.vcxproj and .filters still listed all five
deleted files, so the Visual Studio build fails on a missing source. The Makefile uses
$(wildcard src/*.c), which is precisely why "it compiles" did not catch it -- the blind spot this
landing's own "verified by inventory, not by it compiles" section is about. Landing 11-2 set the
precedent (75e62a9 removed the same two lines for render_ogl.c) and D4 broke it. Fixed, and then
checked further than the finding asked: every file either project lists now exists.

F2 + F3 + F14, THE RETRACTION, AND IT IS THE IMPLEMENTER'S. The docs claimed the engine's
composited layer "flickers continuously" and that every pixel A/B in this gate had passed gate 1
by landing two grabs in the same state. Counting all 36 grabs of the three runs: 33 are at
bit-for-bit the same saturation state (74 495 saturated px). No flicker; the fixture is static
and the earlier gates passed honestly. Two of the three anomalies are SCREEN TEARS -- divergence
begins at row 384 of 768 and at row 392, with everything above byte-identical, and import -window
has no vsync -- which makes the FIRST reading, "a blit caught half done", the one the geometry
supported all along. The docs had deleted that reading as wrong.

The same paragraphs claimed, from a histogram of OUR frame alone, that our lane outputs greyscale
while only the engine's content carries colour. The histogram is right (84.3 % at saturation 0-2,
median 0.0) and the conclusion does not follow: it never compared the engine's frame at the same
points, and this gate had ALREADY refuted it -- an 18-point sample found 5 of 18 points where
OURS is more saturated than the engine's, and recorded "we desaturate each pixel is refuted".
What is established stays narrower: aggregate saturation roughly halved while structure matches,
the signature of a sub-texel sampling offset on dithered terrain art. Both claims withdrawn in
the notes, with the prior refutation restored where a session will meet it.

WHAT SURVIVED AS NEW, and it is about the harness rather than the renderer: all three anomalous
grabs across three runs are in a brB arm, and meas_x.sh runs mainA -> brA -> mainB -> brB, so brB
is always the FOURTH and last launch. Three for three is arm-correlated. Confounded, since brB is
both "last" and "branch" -- but D3's and D4's anomalies came from different branch binaries,
which points at launch order. Untested, and named as untested.

